WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:07.780
It's 2015 and the world's most powerful drug
trafficker, El Chapo, is about to escape from prison.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:10.780
Did you see it?

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:13.920
Right now.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.060
He's gone.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.740
"One of the world's richest, most notorious
drug kingpins broke out of prison."

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.840
"Former cartel leader is considered one of
the most dangerous drug traffickers in the world."

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.680
Joaquin Guzman Loera, better known as "El
Chapo", broke out of his cell by crawling

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.089
out of a hole in the shower.

00:00:40.089 --> 00:00:45.140
The escape of Chapo Guzman translates into
two things:

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.680
More violence for Mexico

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.180
and more drugs coming into the United States.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.120
El Chapo was the leader of the

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.690
Sinaloa Cartel, a murderous criminal organization
that has killed thousands during Mexico's

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.960
violent drug wars.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.300
Their methods are brutal.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:09.370
But violence isn't what makes El Chapo different
from other traffickers.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:15.149
Instead, his legacy has been shaped by two
abilities: transporting more drugs across

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:22.100
the border than anyone else and always finding
a way to escape authorities.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.880
That was, until now.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.780
After being captured again in Mexico, El Chapo
is standing trial in the United States.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.240
It's the latest chapter for the world's most
powerful drug trafficker and to understand

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:40.579
how El Chapo got here, it helps to look at
a single innovation, the one tool that El

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:45.149
Chapo used to transform the drug trade is
the same tool that made him its most elusive

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:46.149
target: tunnels.

00:01:46.149 --> 00:01:46.900
"Tunnels."

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:47.400
"Tunnels."

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:47.900
"Tunnels."

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:48.540
"Through a tunnel."

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.020
"Used by El Chapo."

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:50.720
"Tunnel King."

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:51.680
"Master of tunnels."

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:52.960
[Spanish]: "The king of tunnels."

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.460
"Prince of tunnels."

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.020
"It's a magnificent feat of architecture and
engineering."

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.530
"Being used to smuggle drugs."

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.299
"And it's like looking for a needle in a haystack."

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:03.310
"There's not really any form of technology
right now that can discover these tunnels

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.220
being built."

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:04.880
"The tunnel.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.620
Look at this."

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:15.540
In 1990, federal agents in Douglas, Arizona,
found something new: a sophisticated drug

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.010
tunnel from the US to Mexico.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.210
And when investigators got inside it, they
were amazed by what they saw.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:27.160
The tunnel was 300 feet long and lit by an
electrical system running along the wall.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:34.670
At one end, a lever raised a hydraulic lift
that opened an entrance in the safehouse floor.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.560
One agent said it was "like something out
of a James Bond movie."

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.720
In fact, it had been built by an architect
working for El Chapo.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.790
And agents didn't know it yet, but this was
their first sign of a major shift in the US

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:49.880
drug trade.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.340
In the 1980s, US drug traffic had been dominated
by cocaine coming through the Caribbean, but

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.450
during the next two decades US forces led
a crackdown which changed the drug trade in

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.930
two ways that put El Chapo in the right place
at the right time.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.450
First, drug traffic shifted to routes through
Mexico.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:15.730
Second, as national militaries worked with
the US to destroy crops in South America,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:20.019
traffickers focused on drugs they could grow
elsewhere.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:26.049
The coca plant is only grown in the Andes
mountains, but marijuana can be grown in Mexico,

00:03:26.049 --> 00:03:28.550
especially in mountain ranges along the western
coast.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:35.319
And the same is true for the opium poppy,
the raw ingredient processed to make heroin.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:40.340
For both crops, the densest region of cultivation
includes the state of Sinaloa, the home of El Chapo.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:48.540
El Chapo also had the ideal way to move those
drugs: unlike cocaine, which is odorless and

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:53.360
can be tightly packed into a vehicle, marijuana
is bulky and has a strong smell.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:59.100
But by using his tunnels, El Chapo could get
massive amounts across the border quickly.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.700
By 2010, El Chapo had become the most powerful
trafficker in the country.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.720
And as the Sinaloa Cartel increased their
power, their territory expanded to include

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.909
the entire Western half of the US border.

00:04:13.909 --> 00:04:18.780
As their control spread west, one particular
area became crucial to their operation:

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.790
a suburb of San Diego called Otay Mesa.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.820
There are three reasons why they chose Otay
Mesa, and understanding them gives you a sense

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:30.340
of how sophisticated El Chapo's operation
really was.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:35.980
First, it is near two major transportation
hubs, Tijuana and San Diego, so drugs can

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.760
quickly get to the border and out to dealers
on the other side.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.760
Second, it’s an industrial area across the
border from Tijuana’s airport, meaning there’s

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:51.160
a steady stream of noise and traffic that
make it easy to go undetected.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.140
The third reason, is the soil.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.950
To the west is ocean, where the ground is
too damp,

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:02.230
And to the East the earth rises to become
hard mountainous rock.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:07.200
Otay Mesa is located in between, and soil
maps show a geological sweet spot where the

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.440
soil is soft enough to dig by hand, but strong
enough to support a tunnel.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:15.580
“Yeah, this is the stair system.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.300
it's about a 70 foot drop."

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.800
In November 2010, agents found a tunnel in

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.500
Otay Mesa built by the Sinaloa Cartel.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.620
Inside, electricity powered lighting and a
ventilation system.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:34.130
Along the floor, a rail system allowed workers
to remove rubble during construction and then

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:38.100
transport drugs once they started using it.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.220
Over the next few years, agents found more
massive tunnels.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:48.430
Tunnels like these can take several months
and millions of dollars to build, but they’re

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:49.430
worth it.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:55.980
The tunnels are so big that traffickers can
move multiple tons of drugs at once,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:01.080
As the Sinaloa Cartel continued building in
Otay Mesa, their tunnels got more impressive,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.500
like building an entrance for one beneath
a bathroom floor.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:09.990
El Chapo’s tunnels are dug so deep that
ground penetrating radar can’t detect them

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:15.310
And at ground level, the entrances are hidden
in clever ways.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.560
But El Chapo wasn’t only building tunnels
to smuggle drugs across the border.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:28.340
In 2014, authorities raided his safehouse
in the Sinaloan capital of Culiacan, but El Chapo

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:33.550
escaped by fleeing through a tunnel
hidden beneath his bathtub.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.780
A few days later, authorities tracked him
down in Mazatlan,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:42.340
where El Chapo was captured
and sent to Altiplano maximum security prison.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.301
A year later, he was gone.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.530
This was the largest tunnel ever built by
the Sinaloa Cartel.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.680
It stretched nearly a mile, to a farmhouse
in a nearby field..

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.240
A year before, that field had been empty.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:13.661
Beneath a hole in the floor,
authorities found a motorcycle that El Chapo

00:07:13.661 --> 00:07:16.880
had used to cruise through the tunnel in less
than ten minutes.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.220
In Sinaloa, El Chapo’s supporters celebrated
the spectacular escape.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.260
But his freedom was short lived.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.310
A few months later, authorities broke into
his hideout in Los Mochis, Sinaloa, where

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.880
he was arrested after a bloody gun battle.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:38.750
He escaped through a tunnel hidden behind
his mirror that led into the sewer,

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.370
but authorities caught him a few hours later
when he crawled out of a manhole and tried

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:43.340
to flee town.

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:52.560
This time he was extradited to the US,
where he is on trial in New York City.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.520
Authorities are so serious about security
that they shut down the Brooklyn Bridge when

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:03.380
El Chapo is moved from his Manhattan jail
cell to the court house.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:05.840
But even as he sits in jail, El Chapo’s

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.680
legacy lives on.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:11.940
In 2018, two major tunnels were discovered

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.690
along the US border.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:19.130
In Arizona, one stretched across from Mexico
to the back of an abandoned KFC.

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:23.550
And in California, authorities found an unfinished
tunnel that had been equipped with electricity

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:27.710
and ventilation systems that run on solar
power.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.580
Another piece of his legacy also continues.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:36.780
In 2018, drug violence contributed to more
people being murdered in Mexico than ever before.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.760
But as aspiring kingpins add to the violence
in Mexico, El Chapo is back in his cell.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.920
At least, for now.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.120
Thanks for watching. If you haven't already heard,

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.500
we've launched a paid membership program called the Video Lab.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.080
It's a great way to support our journalism and if you want to join,

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:59.420
you can head over to vox.com/join.

